            "text": "{\"claims\":[{\"type\":\"missing_hyp\",\"location\":\"(∃ L : ℝ, Tendsto (S x) atTop (𝓝 L)) ∧\\n(∀ x ∈ T, Tendsto (S x) atTop (𝓝 (g x))) → ContinuousOn g T\",\"explanation\":\"The informal problem asks to prove that the sequence converges and that its limit function is continuous. In the Lean proposition, convergence appears only in the antecedent of an implication. A proof can assume convergence to g and establish continuity without proving that any sequence converges or constructing a limit function.\",\"prediction\":\"A proof of the original proposition could introduce the implication hypothesis and use its asserted convergence to g, leaving the informal convergence claim unproved.\",\"probe_kind\":\"statement\",\"comparison\":null,\"bridge\":\"parse\",\"obligation\":\"Check that the intended result requires convergence to be established, rather than assuming a pre-existing limit function and proving only its continuity.\"}],\"summary\":\"The formal proposition appears to make convergence an assumption rather than a conclusion.\"}"
